    docs(backlog): whole-library export as JSONL
    
    `cat *.alexandria` cannot work — each sidecar is a complete
    pretty-printed JSON document, so concatenation yields `}{`.
    
    Records that the interchange case may already be solved:
    bibtex_export.export_rows_to_file already writes the whole library as
    one .bib, and CSL-JSON is the JSON-native equivalent. What they cannot
    carry is the point — sidecars have 32 top-level keys and BibTeX keeps
    about eight, losing highlights, notes, marks, structured authorships,
    citations_by_year, the jats block and sha256. So the entry is scoped
    to the lossless case only.
    
    JSONL keeps the property that made `cat` attractive in the first
    place: concatenating two JSONL files is still valid JSONL. One changed
    paper is one changed line, which is what makes it git-reviewable
    rather than a wall of diff. Measured: 168 records, 0.86 MB, longest
    line 30 KB.
    
    Two measurements drive the recommendations. `raw` alone is 33% of the
    bytes and the regenerable fields together are 41%, so excluding them
    halves the file and removes the churn from caches that move whenever a
    citation count does. And a lossless dump carries highlights, notes and
    marks — so the default omits them, with --full for backup, because
    sharing a library and sharing one's annotations are different acts.
    
